# Paths in ~/.claude that chezmoi used to put there and no longer does.
#
# `chezmoi apply` never deletes a file merely because the source stopped managing
# it, and that is true of externals too: dropping an external's entry leaves its
# extracted files behind exactly as deleting a source file leaves the applied copy
# behind. So a departure has to be written down here or it survives in ~ forever on
# every machine that had already applied it.
#
# `.claude/mp-skills` is the clone that Matt Pocock's skills used to arrive in,
# before they became one archive external each in .chezmoiexternal.toml. Nothing
# reads it now and it is the largest single thing in ~/.claude, so it goes.
#
# The first three skills are the review1st/review3rd rename and one deletion.
# Leaving them behind is not cosmetic: the old and new names are both live skills
# with identical content, and an agent choosing between them can choose the stale
# one.
#
# `ask-matt`, `teach`, `to-questionnaire`, `grill-me` and
# `setup-matt-pocock-skills` are five of Matt Pocock's that
# .chezmoiexternal.toml stopped taking -- three for prompt cost, one as a
# duplicate of `grilling`, one as the imperative installer this file replaced;
# see the comment there. `setup-matt-pocock-skills` is the one whose orphan does
# more than take up room: left in place it stays invocable, and what it does when
# invoked is write skills into the directory chezmoi owns. Here the stake is the same as any other
# departure and then some: an external's extracted files outlive its entry, so
# without these lines the skills keep loading their descriptions into every
# session on every machine that had already applied them, which is precisely the
# cost they were removed to avoid.
#
# Why the not-a-symlink guard, when none of the four is a symlink today. An
# unguarded entry deletes whatever it finds, and a symlink here is something
# another tool installed and expects to keep -- run_onchange_after_link-wf-skills.sh
# still links the six wf prompts into this same directory. Deleting one of those
# would be silent and would stay broken, because a run_onchange script does not
# re-run to repair it: its hash has not changed. That is measured, not assumed --
# it is what the old mp-skills link script did before the externals replaced it,
# and it took two applies to see. lstat, not stat, tells the cases apart, since
# stat follows the link and reports "dir". So each line means: remove a real
# directory nothing manages any more; never touch a symlink someone installed.
#
# Removal is processed before externals and before run_onchange_after_* scripts, so
# a machine that has not synced since these left clears them and lands their
# replacements in a single `chezmoi apply`.
#
# Gated on .claudecfg like everything else that writes ~/.claude, and here the
# stake is deletion rather than a wrong file: removing through somebody else's
# bind mount deletes *their* real skills. The flag used to name every container as
# that somebody, on the strength of a devlaunch bind mount that does not exist;
# it now names the mount it actually finds, which is the one a repo's own
# devcontainer asked for.
#
# These are a migration, not a fixture. Once every machine has synced past them
# they are all no-ops and can be dropped.
{{ if .claudecfg -}}
{{   range list
       ".claude/mp-skills"
       ".claude/skills/review1st"
       ".claude/skills/review3rd"
       ".claude/skills/ubiquitous-language"
       ".claude/skills/ask-matt"
       ".claude/skills/teach"
       ".claude/skills/to-questionnaire"
       ".claude/skills/grill-me"
       ".claude/skills/setup-matt-pocock-skills" -}}
{{     $rel := . -}}
{{     with lstat (joinPath $.chezmoi.homeDir $rel) -}}
{{       if ne .type "symlink" }}{{ $rel }}
{{       end -}}
{{     end -}}
{{   end -}}
{{ end -}}

# Dead weight retired 2026-08 (see the PR for the inventory). Same contract as
# above: chezmoi stopped managing these, so without an entry every machine that
# ever applied them keeps its orphan forever. No lstat guard here — unlike
# ~/.claude/skills, nothing links into these paths, and each entry names the
# exact file (or, for tuios, a config dir only chezmoi ever wrote).
#
# .claude/scripts/status-line.py is the python status line that the
# claude-statusline pixi package replaced; settings.json has not named it since.
# Gated on .claudecfg like every other write under ~/.claude: removing through
# somebody else's bind mount deletes *their* file.
{{ if .claudecfg -}}
.claude/scripts/status-line.py
{{ end -}}

# ~/.local/bin is applied on every profile (kinisi included — .xdg does not
# cover it), so these two are ungated. zjcount lost its status-bar slot when the
# command widget went (see zellij-status-bar.kdl); ralph never had a caller or a
# README row and needed a uv nothing provisions.
.local/bin/zjcount
.local/bin/ralph

# Two herdr keybinding helpers that were never pressed. herdr-goto was a
# name-first tab picker on ctrl+g, worth having only while the `/` in herdr's
# own `goto` looked like friction; hnew opened a window on its own named
# session, from kitty bindings a machine reached over SSH has no way to send.
.local/bin/herdr-goto
.local/bin/hnew

# Gated to match the trees' ignore blocks: on a profile where the tree is
# host-mounted (.xdg off) or skipped (.editor off), the host's own apply is the
# one that removes the file, and this apply must not reach through the mount.
{{ if .xdg -}}
# tuios went with the rest of the trial's remnants (pixi env, tui(), the kitty
# comments); the config held only its leader key.
.config/tuios
# herdr's server unit. herdr spawns its own server from the first client, which
# is what upstream does and what this repo now leaves it to do; the unit existed
# to own that server instead. Deleting the file is only half of it -- the enable
# symlink is retired by run_onchange_disable-herdr-server.sh, which has to run
# on machines this file never reaches a second time.
.config/systemd/user/herdr-server.service
{{ if .editor -}}
# LazyVim's inert starter example (`if true then return {} end`).
.config/nvim/lua/plugins/example.lua
{{ end -}}
{{ end -}}
